 ## Overview
-The Hardcoded Transfer Function (HTF) is a classification method that maps scalar volume data values directly to color and opacity using a static, pre-defined 1D lookup table (LUT) [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visualizing scalar fields by assigning RGBA values based solely on intensity, without considering spatial context or gradients [S2, S4]. Unlike gradient-based or texture-based transfer functions, the HTF relies exclusively on the scalar value $x$, making it the simplest and most computationally efficient classification method [S2]. Its primary limitation is the inability to distinguish between different features that share the same scalar value but differ in their local structure or context [S2, S4].
+The Hardcoded Transfer Function (HTF) is a classification method that maps scalar volume data values directly to color and opacity using a static, pre-defined 1D lookup table (LUT) [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of visualizing scalar fields by assigning RGBA values based solely on intensity, without considering spatial context or gradients [S2, S4]. Unlike gradient-based or texture-based transfer functions, the HTF relies exclusively on the scalar value $x$, making it the simplest and most computationally efficient classification method [S2]. Its primary limitation is the inability to distinguish between different features that share the same scalar value but differ in their local structure or context [S2, S4]. It serves as a reference point for other TFs [S2].
 
